course: dictionary
lesson: 7123
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one is meant. The word pelican can name a bird or a road crossing. «Up at the surface, a pelican scoops fish with a stretchy pouch like a net.» The fish and the pouch tell you this pelican is a bird.
explain: «Pelican crossing» is a different meaning. In the UK, a pelican crossing is a place on the road with traffic lights that you control by pressing a button. When you read a word, ask: what is the sentence about? Water and fish, or roads and traffic?
explain: Try your meaning in the sentence to check it. If the bird meaning makes sense, keep it. If it does not, try the road meaning. Good readers do this without thinking.
fact: «Pelican» means a large water bird with a very long beak and a big stretchy pouch under it, used to scoop up fish.
fact: A «pelican crossing» (UK) is a road crossing with traffic lights that people control by pressing a button.
fact: Pelicans live by seas, lakes and rivers in many warm countries, including Australia and the USA.
fact: «Great white pelicans fish as a team, driving fish into shallow water.» Here «shallow» means not very deep.
fact: In science, the pouch works like a fishing net. In road safety, you wait for the green man before you cross.
exercise: «Up at the surface, a pelican scoops fish with a stretchy pouch like a net.» What does a pelican use to scoop fish? | Its tail | *Its stretchy pouch | Its wings | Its eyes
exercise: On a pelican crossing, what do you do to make the lights change? | *Press the button | Walk straight across the road | Wave a flag | Shout loudly at the cars
exercise: «Great white pelicans fish as a team, driving fish into shallow water.» What does shallow mean? | Full of rocks | Deep and dark | Very cold | *Not very deep
test: «A pelican's pouch can hold about 10 litres of water; it drains the water out of the sides of the bill and swallows the fish.» What happens to the water before the pelican swallows? | The water is kept in the pouch forever | *The water drains out of the sides of the bill | The water is drunk | The water turns into fish
test: «Wait by the pelican crossing for the green man.» What does pelican crossing mean here? | A large water bird | A type of fish | A kind of fishing boat | *A road crossing with traffic lights you control with a button
exam: Jayden reads «a pelican scoops fish» and then «wait at the pelican crossing». Why does pelican mean something different in each? | Because pelican is a noun in one and a verb in the other | Because one sentence is in the past tense | Pelican always means the same thing, so Jayden must be wrong | *Because the clues differ: the first is about fish and water, the second is about traffic lights and the road
